1
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:10,075
Penlock turns Passport Prime into an offline signer, backed by paper. This is the KeyOS simulator.

2
00:00:10,075 --> 00:00:14,952
Every launch opens with a warning about protecting your seed phrase.

3
00:00:14,952 --> 00:00:19,013
Home has four jobs: create shares, restore them, read a written phrase, and sign.

4
00:00:19,013 --> 00:00:23,326
Settings holds the network. It starts on signet, and this whole demo stays there.

5
00:00:23,326 --> 00:00:30,109
Mainnet needs a deliberate confirmation. We cancel it here.

6
00:00:30,109 --> 00:00:34,224
Typing a phrase? Each word lands on a numbered line, and typos are flagged as you go.

7
00:00:34,224 --> 00:00:42,668
Or read it from paper. This public sample page comes from Bitcoin Vision.

8
00:00:42,668 --> 00:00:48,365
All 12 handwritten words were read on the device, with no network.

9
00:00:48,365 --> 00:00:52,847
It is always a draft. Check every word against the paper, even if the checksum passes.

10
00:00:52,847 --> 00:00:58,250
Create shares makes three. Any two rebuild the phrase, and one alone reveals nothing.

11
00:00:58,250 --> 00:01:02,203
Each share is a strip to copy by hand, shaded boxes first. The list scrolls.

12
00:01:02,203 --> 00:01:08,678
See all rows opens the whole share on one screen, with nothing to scroll.

13
00:01:08,678 --> 00:01:12,970
Mark a share copied and the next one opens, with the count in the corner.

14
00:01:12,970 --> 00:01:18,378
Saving a share as an image always asks first.

15
00:01:18,378 --> 00:01:24,491
It lands in a penlock folder, named with its number, date and time.

16
00:01:24,491 --> 00:01:35,130
A second saved share gets a stronger warning, because two shares rebuild the seed.

17
00:01:35,130 --> 00:01:38,582
Copying the last share finishes the job.

18
00:01:38,582 --> 00:01:47,602
Restoring works in any order. Tap a share and pick its strip. Here, the third share first.

19
00:01:47,602 --> 00:01:54,225
Now the second share. The camera picture is blanked in this recording.

20
00:01:54,225 --> 00:01:57,869
Two strips rebuild the phrase. It stays hidden until you choose to see it.

21
00:01:57,869 --> 00:02:04,883
Slide to reveal the words, and tap Hide to cover them again.

22
00:02:04,883 --> 00:02:08,207
Penlock never builds a transaction. Your wallet hands it a PSBT, pasted in this simulator.

23
00:02:08,207 --> 00:02:15,296
You check the destination, change and fee here, then slide to sign.

24
00:02:15,296 --> 00:02:21,044
Signed with the rebuilt seed. Scan this QR with an online device to broadcast it.

25
00:02:21,044 --> 00:02:25,976
Sign a transaction offers three ways to load a key.

26
00:02:25,976 --> 00:02:35,447
A scanned seed phrase can sign directly, without going through shares.

27
00:02:35,447 --> 00:02:40,096
A PSBT from someone else's coins is refused, and the seed is dropped from memory.

28
00:02:40,096 --> 00:02:45,795
The real advantage is MuSig2: two devices, two shares, and neither ever sees the other's.

29
00:02:45,795 --> 00:02:50,342
One share becomes its own signing key. A Passport scans the strip, and paste stands in here.

30
00:02:50,342 --> 00:02:55,676
Show my key gives your cosigner a public key as a QR code. It can't spend.

31
00:02:55,676 --> 00:02:59,936
The second signer is a laptop tool holding share 2, standing in for a phone or another Passport.

32
00:02:59,936 --> 00:03:04,030
Now it is 2-of-2. Sign and Receive unlock.

33
00:03:04,030 --> 00:03:13,441
Both devices derive the same shared address, without exchanging anything secret.

34
00:03:13,441 --> 00:03:20,470
Each signer reviews the same spend. This one has no change, so all but the fee goes out.

35
00:03:20,470 --> 00:03:25,561
Round 1: each signer adds a one-time nonce. On a Passport this moves by QR code.

36
00:03:25,561 --> 00:03:30,642
Round 2: each signer adds a partial signature, checked before anything is joined.

37
00:03:30,642 --> 00:03:36,416
They join into one 64-byte signature. On chain it looks like any single-key spend.

38
00:03:36,416 --> 00:03:45,891
With a signer open, Home shows its key, and Settings locks the network.

39
00:03:45,891 --> 00:03:53,028
Lock signer drops the key. Nothing stays behind.

